EXT. HARBOR REGATTA COURSE - DAY

Grey chop under a grey sky. White sails tilt in the distance like paper cutouts. A committee boat bobs near the starting line, a red flag snapping on its stern.

On the committee boat's deck stands WESLEY KOVALENKO (50s, windbreaker, binoculars, a clipboard sheathed in plastic), a radio in his fist. A hundred yards off, in a battered single-handed dinghy, AMINATA DRUMMOND (20s, wetsuit, spray-stung face) trims a mainsheet. Her knuckles are raw.

                    WESLEY
                (radio)
          Aminata, you're early to the line.

                    AMINATA (V.O.)
                (radio, crackling)
          I know.

                    WESLEY
                (radio)
          Fall off. Tack. You've got ninety seconds.

                    AMINATA (V.O.)
                (radio)
          I'm fine.

She isn't. The boat heels. She leans out, boots hooked under the strap, and hauls the sail in. The dinghy skims, a hull slapping the crest of each wave.

Wesley lifts the binoculars. Clenches his jaw.

                    WESLEY
                (radio)
          Forty seconds. Come about.

She throws the tiller. The boom swings across, clips her shoulder, and sends her stumbling into the cockpit while the sail luffs. Wesley mutters to himself, dropping the binoculars to his chest, climbing up onto the rail with one hand on the rigging.

                    WESLEY (CONT'D)
          Come on, come on.

                    AMINATA (V.O.)
                (radio)
          I'm up. I'm up.

She scrambles upright, regains the tiller, swings the boat in a tight arc, and the sail fills with a crack like a rifle shot.

A horn blasts across the harbor. The fleet surges.

                    WESLEY
                (radio)
          And you're off. Four seconds late.

                    AMINATA (V.O.)
                (radio)
          Is that a penalty?

                    WESLEY
                (radio)
          It's a lesson.

She laughs, a harsh gasp, and leans out. The dinghy gains speed, cutting through the pack. Wesley watches her go, lowers the radio, and for a moment lets his face show everything.

Then he lifts it again.

                    WESLEY (CONT'D)
                (radio)
          Mark one in half a mile. Keep it low.

                    AMINATA (V.O.)
                (radio)
          Copy.

The first mark swings into view, a yellow buoy riding the swell. Half the fleet crowds toward it. She lines up wide, takes the outside lane, and Wesley mutters again.

                    WESLEY (CONT'D)
          Don't do it.

She does it. She cuts inside at the last second, clipping the buoy's rope with her stern. Boats scatter. A whistle shrieks. Wesley sits down on the rail, head in his hands.

                    WESLEY (CONT'D)
                (radio, flat)
          Aminata.

                    AMINATA (V.O.)
                (radio)
          I know. I touched the mark.

                    WESLEY
                (radio)
          Do your turns.

                    AMINATA (V.O.)
                (radio)
          I'm doing them.

The dinghy spins two full circles on the grey water, clean as a compass drawing. The pack, now far ahead, shrinks toward the horizon.

                    WESLEY
                (radio)
          You'll finish last.

                    AMINATA (V.O.)
                (radio)
          I'll finish.

He smiles, finally. Not at the radio, at the sea.

                    WESLEY
                (radio)
          That's the whole sport.
